WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.073
Safe request construction means building method, path, query parameters,
headers, body, content type, and optional file uploads through controlled
patterns.

2
00:00:09.073 --> 00:00:19.471
The client should know which HTTP methods are allowed for each operation,
which path shapes are expected, which headers are required, and which
fields can appear in JSON or form payloads.

3
00:00:19.471 --> 00:00:29.339
Dynamic assembly is sometimes necessary, but untrusted input should not
freely control URLs, header names, authentication material, request
methods, or protocol behavior.

4
00:00:29.339 --> 00:00:32.849
Validation should happen before data leaves the application.

5
00:00:32.849 --> 00:00:37.883
Check type, shape, length, format, allowed values, tenant context, and
business intent.

6
00:00:37.883 --> 00:00:51.725
If a user-controlled field becomes part of a query parameter, body field,
file name, metadata value, or downstream action, the client should enforce
the contract locally rather than assuming the remote API will reject
unsafe or unintended values.

7
00:00:51.725 --> 00:00:56.692
Remote validation is useful, but it is not a substitute for protecting
your own workflow.

8
00:00:56.692 --> 00:01:00.732
Data minimization is a security control, not only a privacy preference.

9
00:01:00.732 --> 00:01:04.441
Clients should send only the data needed for the approved purpose.

10
00:01:04.441 --> 00:01:11.063
Avoid forwarding complete local records when the remote service only needs
an identifier, status, or specific field.

11
00:01:11.063 --> 00:01:17.488
Avoid including secrets, internal notes, debug fields, unnecessary user
attributes, or unrelated payload data.

12
00:01:17.488 --> 00:01:25.766
Minimization reduces exposure if the destination logs requests, has
broader access, is misconfigured, or later changes how it uses received
data.

13
00:01:25.766 --> 00:01:31.727
Request signing and canonicalization concepts can be useful for some APIs,
but they require consistency.

14
00:01:31.727 --> 00:01:39.409
The client should be clear about exactly what is signed, how timestamps or
nonces are used, and how retries interact with signed requests.

15
00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:46.363
Even when a library handles signing, callers should avoid modifying signed
headers or paths after the signature is created.

16
00:01:46.363 --> 00:01:52.920
The practical goal is predictable requests whose security meaning matches
the operation the application intended.
